# =============================================================================
# configuration.properties.example
#
# Committed TEMPLATE for the OPTIONAL runtime configuration file
# `configuration.properties`, which `[.gitignore:L3]` keeps untracked. That
# single ignore line is the whole of the evidence that the pre-migration
# Java/Maven project had a runtime configuration contract at all, which is why
# preserving it is a named migration goal (AAP O9, "Configuration parity"): the
# contract survives as a git-ignored runtime file PLUS this committed template,
# complemented by the committed `.env.example`.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#
#     cp configuration.properties.example configuration.properties
#
# Then edit the copy. The copy is never committed; this template always is.
#
# EVERY KEY IS OPTIONAL, AND SO IS THE WHOLE FILE
#
# Because `[.gitignore:L3]` untracks it, `configuration.properties` is NEVER
# present in a fresh checkout, so its absence is the NORMAL case rather than an
# error. Every key below has a working hard-coded default in `app/config.py`;
# any single key may be deleted, and the whole file may be deleted outright -
# the Flask application still starts and the test harness still runs.
# `app/utils/properties.py` is the one and only reader of this file and it
# degrades silently when the file is absent, unreadable or malformed.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A SECRET HERE
#
# This template is tracked by git, so it must never contain a real credential,
# password, token, API key or private key - not even temporarily. Keep local
# secrets in your untracked `configuration.properties` copy or in the process
# environment. Secrets management and credential vaulting are out of scope for
# this project: templates document KEYS, never real VALUES.
#
# FILE FORMAT - Java `.properties`, not INI
#
#   * `key=value`, one pair per line. Values are NOT quoted; a quote character
#     would become part of the value.
#   * No `[section]` header. `app/utils/properties.py` synthesizes one in memory
#     before handing the text to the standard library's `configparser`, so a
#     header written here would be a second, nested header and every key under
#     it would become unreachable. Do not add one.
#   * Comment markers are `#` (used throughout) and `!`. A leading `;` is not a
#     Java comment marker and would be parsed as data.
#   * Keys are case-sensitive, exactly as `java.util.Properties` treats them.
#   * No trailing whitespace after a value: it is preserved verbatim and would
#     silently break an exact comparison such as the French assertion literal in
#     section 7.
#   * UTF-8, LF line endings, pure ASCII, trailing newline.
#
# Parsing this format costs ZERO extra dependencies - `configparser` plus the
# synthesized section header handles it - which is why no `javaproperties`-style
# package appears in `requirements.txt`.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE SITS IN THE PRECEDENCE CHAIN (AAP 0.3.1)
#
#     explicit constructor argument
#       -> environment variable
#         -> .env file
#           -> configuration.properties          <-- THIS FILE (fourth)
#             -> hard-coded default in app/config.py
#
# Highest wins. This file is FOURTH of five: an environment variable or a `.env`
# entry overrides anything set here, and anything set here in turn overrides the
# hard-coded default. Only an explicit constructor argument outranks the
# environment.
#
# SEE ALSO
#
#   .env.example              The same settings expressed as environment
#                             overrides. Every key below has a counterpart
#                             there and the default values agree, key for key.
#   docs/configuration.md     The precedence chain and every setting's source.
#   docs/migration-parity.md  The authoritative D1-D9 defect register.
#
# EVERY VALUE BELOW IS THE SOURCE PROJECT'S OWN VALUE, cited inline against the
# file and line it came from. Values are data, not opportunities to modernise
# (AAP Rule T1). Several of them look wrong and ARE wrong: they are preserved
# deliberately (AAP Rule T4) and each one says so, because an uncommented
# `LogOut` or `true` is exactly the kind of thing a future maintainer "fixes",
# thereby breaking the parity criteria this port is accepted against.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Repository clone - port of Jenkins stage 'Clone code' [Jenkins:L2-L4]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D7 - two contradictory repository URLs.
# The pipeline clones https://github.com/BalamiRR/Upgenix-QA.git [Jenkins:L3],
# while the README instructs cloning
# https://github.com/BalamiRR/Testinium-QA.git [README.md:L59].
# Per AAP AMB-7 the PIPELINE value is the runtime default: executable
# configuration outranks prose when the two disagree, and the Jira key prefix
# `UPGN` [README.md:L115] agrees with the pipeline URL. Both strings are
# retained rather than silently unified, so no information is lost.
clone.url=https://github.com/BalamiRR/Upgenix-QA.git

# The README's URL, offered here as a commented-out ALTERNATIVE. Uncomment this
# line and comment the one above to clone the repository the README documents.
#clone.url=https://github.com/BalamiRR/Testinium-QA.git

# The README's URL also kept as a value in its own right, so the discrepancy
# stays machine-readable and the configuration endpoint can report both.
# Source: [README.md:L59]. Mirrors CLONE_URL_DOCUMENTED in `.env.example`.
clone.url.documented=https://github.com/BalamiRR/Testinium-QA.git

# The pipeline's `git '<url>'` step [Jenkins:L3] passes no explicit ref, so it
# checks out the repository's default branch.
clone.branch=main

# Working-copy destination, relative to the repository root. ADDITIVE: the
# Groovy `git` step wrote into the Jenkins workspace, which has no analogue in
# a plain Python process.
clone.dir=workspace

# ADDITIVE but REQUIRED. The Groovy `git` step is not a bare clone - it also
# handles workspace cleanup and polling - so `app/services/clone_service.py`
# replaces it with an explicit argument-list subprocess call that must never
# block for ever. Seconds.
clone.timeout.seconds=300


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Test execution - port of Jenkins stage 'Run tests' [Jenkins:L6-L11] and of
#    the Maven Surefire configuration [pom.xml:L21-L29]
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D2 - the default tag expression selects ZERO scenarios.
# Faithful port of the documented runner's tags = "@LogOut" [README.md:L87];
# pytest-bdd turns each Gherkin tag into a pytest mark and strips the leading
# `@`, so the value carries no `@`.
# No scenario carries that tag. The feature file's only tags are @Login,
# @UPGN-286, @UPGN-287, @UPGN-288, @SalesManager and @PosManager, so a default
# run deselects every scenario and pytest exits with code 5, "no tests ran".
# Exit code 5 MUST be reported as a SUCCESSFUL zero-scenario run: the source
# pipeline went green in exactly this situation, and mapping 5 to failure would
# make the port fail where the original succeeded.
# Set this key to an empty value (`tag.expression=`) to run the whole suite.
tag.expression=LogOut

# PRESERVED DEFECT D3 - the build can never fail.
# Direct port of <testFailureIgnore>true</testFailureIgnore> [pom.xml:L25].
# While this is true, pytest exit code 1 (tests collected, some failed) and
# exit code 5 (nothing selected) are BOTH non-gating; only 2 (interrupted),
# 3 (internal error) and 4 (usage error) are hard failures. The six report
# thresholds in section 3 are all -1, which the publisher treats as "no
# threshold", so they gate nothing either, and report generation runs even
# after a FAILED test stage because the report stage sits in a
# finally-equivalent path.
# Set to false to make test failures gate the pipeline - a deliberate
# deviation from source behaviour; see docs/migration-parity.md.
ignore.test.failures=true

# Port of <parallel>methods</parallel> plus
# <useUnlimitedThreads>true</useUnlimitedThreads> [pom.xml:L22-L23] onto
# pytest-xdist worker allocation. `logical` lets xdist size the pool from the
# machine, which is the closest available analogue of "unlimited threads".
pytest.workers=logical

# PRESERVED-BUT-DISABLED tuning default. pom.xml keeps its thread count
# commented out - `<!-- <threadCount>4</threadCount> -->` [pom.xml:L24] - and so
# does this template, exactly (AAP O7). Uncomment to pin four workers.
#pytest.workers=4

# ADDITIVE guard rail so a hung browser session cannot stall a run for ever.
# Seconds; applies to the whole pytest invocation.
test.timeout.seconds=1800


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Report publication - port of Jenkins stage 'Generate report'
#    [Jenkins:L13-L15]
#
# Every value in this section is read straight off the `cucumber` publisher
# invocation [Jenkins:L15], which the updated pipeline preserves
# byte-identically. `app/reporting/thresholds.py` freezes the same constants in
# Python, and parity criterion V4 asserts that they still match.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D3, continued - all six thresholds are -1, which the
# publisher treats as "no threshold". Nothing here can ever fail a build.
report.failed.features.number=-1
report.failed.scenarios.number=-1
report.failed.steps.number=-1
report.pending.steps.number=-1
report.skipped.steps.number=-1
report.undefined.steps.number=-1

# sortingMethod: 'ALPHABETICAL' [Jenkins:L15]. A no-op while there is exactly
# one feature, but implemented anyway so the behaviour survives the addition of
# further features instead of becoming a latent divergence.
report.sorting.method=ALPHABETICAL

# fileIncludePattern: '**/*.json' [Jenkins:L15], preserved VERBATIM AS DATA.
# This is the publisher's own glob, not a transformation pattern: never rewrite
# it. It is also the reason the artifact root in section 4 keeps its
# Java-flavoured name - leaving the reports under `target/` means this contract
# needs no edit at all.
report.file.include.pattern=**/*.json


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Artifact layout - the four report artifacts declared by the documented
#    runner's plugin list [README.md:L78-L81], plus the shots directories
#    [README.md:L42-L43] and the retained Surefire report directory
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Artifact root. AAP 0.3.1 writes it as `target/` in directory notation; the
# value here is the bare directory NAME so that it compares equal, byte for
# byte, to the committed constants app/utils/paths.py::TARGET_DIR_NAME and
# TARGET_DIR in `.env.example`. Every derived path below is rooted at `target/`
# verbatim. Do NOT rename this directory: the publisher glob '**/*.json' in
# section 3 depends on it, and renaming it is precisely the change that would
# break the CI contract the migration went out of its way to keep.
target.dir=target

# json:target/cucumber.json - the one artifact the publisher glob matches.
# Its writer does NOT create this parent directory, so the `target/` tree must
# already exist before a run; app/utils/paths.py, the Makefile `test` target and
# tests/conftest.py each guarantee that independently.
cucumber.json.path=target/cucumber.json

# html:target/cucumber-reports.html - served as a static artifact, never
# re-rendered, so it cannot diverge from what the source toolchain produced.
cucumber.html.path=target/cucumber-reports.html

# rerun:target/rerun.txt - one `<feature-uri>:<scenario-line>` entry per failing
# scenario, derived deterministically from the JSON report so the manifest can
# never drift away from it.
rerun.txt.path=target/rerun.txt

# me.jvt.cucumber.report.PrettyReports:target/cucumber - produced by
# post-processing target/cucumber.json, exactly as the Java reporting plugin
# [pom.xml:L66-L70] rendered its directory from the JSON rather than from
# terminal output.
pretty.reports.dir=target/cucumber

# Screen shots and error shots [README.md:L42-L43]. Both sit under the artifact
# root so `make clean`, the port of `mvn clean`, wipes them with everything else.
screenshots.dir=target/screenshots
error.shots.dir=target/error-shots

# Surefire's report directory name is retained for report-consumer parity, so
# any downstream consumer keyed on this path keeps finding content. It is
# populated by the JUnit-XML output the runner requests.
surefire.reports.dir=target/surefire-reports


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Screen shots and error shots [README.md:L42-L43]
#
# The README states the project generates screen shots for your tests "if you
# enable it", and generates error shots for failed test cases as well. The two
# defaults below follow that wording literally.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Opt-in, exactly as "if you enable it" describes: shots of passing steps are
# off by default.
screenshots.enabled=false

# Error shots for failed test cases are described unconditionally, so they are
# on by default. Captured by the screenshot-on-failure hook in
# tests/step_defs/conftest.py.
error.shots.enabled=true


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Browser automation - Selenium WebDriver
#
# The source project's prerequisite list asked for a browser driver with its
# class path already set [README.md:L53]; the keys below replace that manual
# step with configuration.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The browser to drive. Deliberately single-valued: a cross-browser and cross-OS
# test matrix is out of scope, so this is one browser and not a browser LIST.
browser=chrome

# ADDITIVE. Headless by default because the build environment has no display.
headless=true

# Driver provisioning strategy. Selenium 4 resolves drivers itself through
# Selenium Manager; the `webdriver-manager` distribution is retained as the
# one-to-one counterpart of io.github.bonigarcia:webdrivermanager
# [pom.xml:L42-L46]. Accepted values: selenium-manager | webdriver-manager
driver.manager=selenium-manager

# ADDITIVE wait budgets, in seconds. tests/pages/base_page.py uses EXPLICIT
# waits; the implicit wait is kept only as a backstop, and mixing the two
# aggressively is why the explicit budget is the larger of the pair.
implicit.wait.seconds=10
explicit.wait.seconds=30
page.load.timeout.seconds=60

# Base URL of the EXTERNAL application under test - the French-language site the
# login scenarios drive. Deliberately left UNSET and commented out: no address
# for it exists anywhere in the source project, and inventing one is forbidden
# (AAP Rule T6). The only two URLs in evidence are the two git remotes in
# section 1, and neither is the application under test. That application is
# external, unmodifiable and unreachable from CI, so the browser-driven
# scenarios cannot run end to end here; the limitation is recorded as risk R6
# rather than papered over.
# Uncomment and supply your own environment's address to run them locally.
# Mirrors BASE_URL in `.env.example`, which documents it with an empty value.
#base.url=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Behaviour-parity literals
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D5 - the localisation and the comment disagree.
# The feature file's explanatory comment says the expected text is
# "Please fill out this field" [README.md:L130], while the ASSERTION five lines
# later expects the French "Veuillez renseigner ce champ." [README.md:L135].
# The assertion is the executable truth, so the French string is the value here.
# It is 29 bytes and pure ASCII: French-language, but not multi-byte. Compare it
# EXACTLY - do not translate it to match the English comment, do not drop the
# trailing period, and do not normalise it in any way.
expected.empty.field.message=Veuillez renseigner ce champ.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Logging
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Standard-library level name: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL.
# Structured logging only - the application never prints.
log.level=INFO

# Destination for the structured log. The `.log` suffix is deliberate: the
# repository has ignored `*.log` since before the migration [.gitignore:L6], so
# this path stays untracked without any new ignore rule being needed.
log.file=logs/testinium-qa.log


# =============================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS FILE
#
#   SECRET_KEY, APP_CONFIG, APP_HOST, APP_PORT, FLASK_APP, FLASK_DEBUG
#       Process- and deployment-level settings that belong in the environment or
#       in `.env`. SECRET_KEY in particular must never be committed anywhere.
#   CONFIGURATION_PROPERTIES_PATH
#       It LOCATES this file, so setting it inside this file cannot work. It is
#       an environment-only override; see `.env.example`.
#   CLONE_INDEX
#       Shifts host-global resources - listen port, compose project name - so
#       that parallel clones do not collide. Environment-only by design.
#   Test account credentials
#       The only account data in evidence lives in the feature file's Examples
#       tables and stays there. It is test data for the ported scenarios, not
#       configuration, and it is deliberately not duplicated here.
#   Cross-browser / cross-OS matrix keys
#       Out of scope. app/utils/platform_exec.py preserves the pipeline's
#       isUnix() branch [Jenkins:L7-L11] and nothing more.
#   Keys for the three unexercised user roles
#       Out of scope. Only PosManager and SalesManager appear in the
#       specification [README.md:L106-L109]; the remaining three of five roles
#       are named nowhere and are not invented here.
# =============================================================================
